\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb4 Official Review\cf5 \cb3 \strokec5 by\'a0Reviewer KuvT09 May 2026, 03:22 (modified: 22 May 2026, 06:48)Program Chairs, Area Chairs, Reviewers Submitted, Authors, Reviewer KuvT{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openreview.net/revisions?id=7lNwPErhCT"}}{\fldrslt \cf6 \strokec6 Revisions}}\cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Summary:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb3 The paper identifies asymmetry in attention. They state that according to the Johnson-Lindenstrauss lemma, selecting\'a0\'a0can be done using\'a0\'a0dimensions. The authors propose to use truncated SVD to factorize\'a0\'a0and absort\'a0\'a0in query projection. They demonstrate with experiments the efficacy of their method.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Reasons To Accept:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The core insight is clean and well-motivated. The Johnson-Lindenstrauss framing gives principled grounding to what could otherwise read as an empirical hack. The "free absorption" trick is elegant. Because queries are computed fresh each step and never cached, pushing the B factor into\'a0\'a0costs nothing at inference time while shrinking the cache.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 Three paths (zero-cost SVD at d/2, SVD+FT at d/4, train-from-scratch) with clear tradeoffs, plus measured H100 throughput showing 1.44x decode speedup at batch 32. The composability analysis with GQA (84.4% combined savings) and the comparison framing MLA's\'a0\'a0= 512 for 128 heads as "the thin-keys insight already embedded" is a thoughtful contextualization rather than a competitive claim.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Reasons To Reject:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The theoretical motivation, while elegant, is loose. JL gives an existence result for random projections preserving distances but doesn't directly establish that learned QK projections concentrate in O(log N) dimensions, or that the "effective N" of language modeling is small.\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 GSM8K results are concerning and partially papered over. At rank 256, GSM8K drops 13.7% with WikiText-103 fine-tuning. The recovery via in-domain GSM8K fine-tuning (closing the gap to -1.2%) is reassuring but raises the question: does compression preferentially damage capabilities that require precise multi-step reasoning, in ways that only become visible on harder benchmarks?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Rating:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \strokec7 \'a06: Marginally above acceptance threshold\cb1 \

