\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb4 Official Review\cf5 \cb3 \strokec5 by\'a0Reviewer GmAj09 May 2026, 02:53 (modified: 22 May 2026, 06:48)Program Chairs, Area Chairs, Reviewers Submitted, Authors, Reviewer GmAj{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openreview.net/revisions?id=Gpc9WzKBlB"}}{\fldrslt \cf6 \strokec6 Revisions}}\cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Summary:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb3 This paper presents a method for compressing the Key cache for efficient LLM attention. They aim to exploit the property of attention that the query/key comparison is just used to obtain scalar scores to rank the values, and distinguishing between these values requires only a logarithmic amount of dimensions. They use this to design a key cache compression strategy that projects the keys to a low-rank subspace (using an offline SVD + projection merging method to avoid inference overhead). There approach is composable with GQA + existing compression methods.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Reasons To Accept:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 This paper provides in-depth analysis showing how the keys exhibit a distinct low-rank structure (which is not apparent in the values), and present a detailed theoretical argument as to why low-rank keys should be sufficient for ranking value vectors\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 Their approach can be composed with quantization / GQA\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Reasons To Reject:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The argument for their approach over GQA is weak - they state that they update the model using ~3 epochs on <1% of pretraining data; however, pooling + similar lightweight continued training can be used for adapting pretrained full-attention models to GQA.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 Their approach only reduces the size of the key cache, leaving the value cache uncompressed\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The comparison with MLA is not convincing - MLA-based models can attain greater savings, and they do not show convincing evidence their method outperforms MLA-based models with training.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Questions To Authors:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 How is RoPE supported with the argument that you can factor out part of the W_k weight matrix and fuse it with W_q?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

