\f1\b0 \cf2 \cb4 Official Review\cf5 \cb3 \strokec5 by\'a0Reviewer S4Dn08 May 2026, 13:42 (modified: 22 May 2026, 06:48)Program Chairs, Area Chairs, Reviewers Submitted, Authors, Reviewer S4Dn{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openreview.net/revisions?id=Uf5tYzQB7n"}}{\fldrslt \cf6 \strokec6 Revisions}}\cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Summary:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb3 This paper is flawed on theory, experiments and presentation. The theoretical motivation lacks rigor and it is not clear why the invoked lemma relates deeply to the problem. The proposed method is inherently limited as the maximal achievable KV cache compression rate is 50%, the value vectors being left uncompressed. The authors do not conduct clear baseline comparisons on language modeling performance against directly relevant baselines, and they do not test their method on long-context evaluation, i.e. where the memory benefits are expected to matter. It seems to me that there is no technical setup where this method would be relevant.\cb1 \
\cb3 A positive aspect of the paper consists in showing that performing SVD on key representations is not very harmful for model performance.\cb1 \
\cb3 Finally, the presentation is rather poor, as agentic / LLM style is recognizable through most of the sentences, clear information is missing from the paragraphs and the accumulation of tables makes the results hard to parse.\cb1 \
\cb3 I recommend resubmitting the paper to another venue with major revisions.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Reasons To Accept:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 Reducing the KV cache footprint on memory is an important research question in Transformers-based LLMs.\cb1 \
\ls8\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The observation that performing a SVD of key projection on a trained model does not signifcantly affect performance is insightful about the structure of query and key vectors.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Reasons To Reject:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \cb1 \strokec7 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf7 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 From the start, the method presented in this paper is inherently limited by the fact that its maximal potential for total KV cache memory reduction is a\'a02 compression level. Most KV cache compression methods (eviction methods such H2O, SnapKV, KeyDiff which would be important to cite, quantization methods, ...) aim for a wider range of compression ratios, and often report successful x4-x32 compression rates with negligible performance drops similar to what is reported in the paper.\cb1 \
\ls9\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The theoretical motivation is not convincing. Even though the connection to the Johnson\'96Lindenstrauss lemma seems relevant at first glance, the authors never validate the relevance of the log(N) observation on the key cache compression method. Quite the opposite, in Table 7, it appears that\'a0\'a0leads to poor performance, while\'a0. The Johnson\'96Lindenstrauss lemma uses an\'a0\'a0formulation for distance preservation, which is not discussed in the paper. The Johnson\'96Lindenstrauss lemma also only motivates for a projection to a smaller space, but it does not justify the use of SVD.\cb1 \
\ls9\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 Two crucial kinds of experiments are missing. First, there is no evaluation of the method on long context scenarii (e.g. RULER, LongBench, or just long-context PPL), where memory-related interventions may have more impact on the performance. This kind of evaluation is absolutely necessary when evaluating KV cache compression methods. Second, there is no comparison to baselines in terms of performance, whether in the case of direct use on a trained model (e.g. KV cache eviction methods) or for pretraining (GQA or MLA). In both cases, it appears to me that it it is not clear that the proposed method would beat its counterparts in terms of performance and/or achievable compression ratios.\cb1 \
\ls9\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 Many experimental choices are outdated: using OpenWebText is not relevant in 2026. Most recent papers use FineWeb / FineWeb-Edu / DCLM / Nemotron-CC as pre-training data, which would potentially allow non-random performance on MMLU and GSM8k.\cb1 \
\ls9\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 This paper is poorly written and presentation could be significantly improved. Most sentences share the same "affirmation :/-- argument" pattern, which is characteristic of LLMs -- the paper contains 51 em-dashes. This is not only an issue of style or LLM usage, but it simply makes the paper redundant and hard to read. The experimental results are reported in hard-to-parse tables. It is never mentioned explicitly in the Experiment section paragraphs that the metric in most tables is perplexity, and it is sometimes not clear on what the perplexity is measured (table 1).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Rating:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \strokec7 \'a03: Clear rejection\cb1 \

\f0\b \cf8 \cb3 \strokec8 Confidence:
\f1\b0 \cf7 \strokec7 \'a05: The reviewer is absolutely certain that the evaluation is correct and very familiar with the relevant literature\cb1 \

